      "content": "I understand your request for a professional, physiology-focused explanation without the goblin metaphors. I apologize for the previous turn; I will strictly adhere to the biological mechanisms you need for your client.\n\nHere is the professional breakdown of the mechanisms you requested:\n\n**1. Leucine and the mTOR Pathway**\nLeucine acts as a primary activator of the mechanistic Target of Rapamycin Complex 1 (mTORC1). The process begins when leucine binds to the Rag GTPase complex (specifically RagA and RagB), which facilitates the translocation of mTORC1 from the cytoplasm to the lysosomal surface. Once at the lysosome, mTORC1 phosphorylates downstream effectors such as S6K1 (ribosomal protein S6 kinase) and 4E-BP1 (eukaryotic translation initiation factor 4E-binding protein). This phosphorylation event releases 4E-BP1, allowing it to bind eIF4E and initiate cap-dependent translation, while S6K1 promotes ribosome biogenesis and protein synthesis. Crucially, this pathway is highly sensitive to the concentration of leucine; typically, a threshold of approximately",
